  "id": "midnight",
  "name": "Midnight",
  "tagline": "A kilometre of floodlit straight, two hairpins, and esses you take on trust",
  "difficulty": "Dark",
  "notes": [
    "A night circuit, which the renderer could not express until the sky fill became a per-track value. The sun is a moon: 0.35 of a very blue light from low in the north, against a fill of 0.10. That ratio is the whole thing -- at the old fixed 1.15 fill there is no such thing as night, only a dark-blue noon.",
    "Fog pulled in hard, 60 m to 340 m. It is doing two jobs: it is what darkness actually looks like at speed, and it hides the fact that the beams only reach about 100 m, so the world ending at 340 m reads as night rather than as a draw distance.",
    "Grip 0.9 rather than the Mediterranean 1.05 this started from: cold tarmac and a bit of damp off the night air. Still the second-highest tarmac grip in the game, because the difficulty here should come from not being able to see, not from the car sliding.",
    "Traced from a sequence of straights and arcs rather than drawn by hand, because the two things that decide whether a lap is worth driving -- corner RADIUS and straight LENGTH -- are invisible in a list of coordinates. Turn angles sum to 360 degrees, which fixes every heading, which makes the closure linear in the straight lengths; two of them are left free and solved exactly. Closure error 0.0 m.",
    "The shape: a 900 m floodlit pit straight into a 42 m hairpin, a fast left sweeper, an ess pair at 95 m either way, a slow 60 m right onto the second-longest straight, a flat-out 420 m kink, then a 40 m hairpin, and a long 150 m right that carries you back onto the straight. The first version of this circuit was a copy of Mediterranean and had five corners, none tighter than 159 m -- a circle, and about as interesting to drive as one.",
    "Elevation is invented: two gentle waves over the lap, kept small enough not to fight the terrain envelope.",
    "Headlights default ON here. L still toggles them, and turning them off is worth doing once."
  ],
